# Token efficiency

Treat context and tool output as a limited budget. Do not burn tokens on work the user did not ask for.

## Before reading or searching

- Use the smallest tool that answers the question: `grep` or a targeted read before opening whole files or launching explore agents.
- Do not re-read files already in context unless they changed.
- For large files (e.g. `data/cfa-data.ts`), read only the section you need — never the full file unless unavoidable.
- One discovery pass, then act. Avoid repeated searches for the same symbol or pattern.

## While working

- Prefer a focused diff over refactors, extra abstractions, or drive-by fixes.
- Do not run build, test, or lint unless the change warrants it — batch verification when possible.
- Do not start background servers or long polls unless the user needs live output.
- Skip subagents for tasks solvable with one or two direct tool calls.

## In responses

- Be concise. No restating the user's question, no recap of steps already taken, no filler suggestions at the end.
- Cite code with line ranges; do not paste entire files into chat.
- Explain only what changed and why — skip obvious implementation narration.

## When blocked

- Ask one clear question instead of exploring broadly on speculation.
- If a first approach fails, try one well-reasoned alternative before escalating scope.
